  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})={cot}^{2}\u2061x$ and $L({y}_{2})=3{csc}^{2}\u2061x-4$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(4{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})?$\n\n*Hint: Use ${csc}^{2}\u2061\\theta =1+{cot}^{2}\u2061\\theta .$*",
  "worked_solution": "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(4{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =4L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {cot}^{2}\u2061x-(3{csc}^{2}\u2061x-4) \\\\  & =4{cot}^{2}\u2061x+4-3{csc}^{2}\u2061x \\\\  & =4(1+{cot}^{2}\u2061x)-3{csc}^{2}\u2061x \\\\  & =4{csc}^{2}\u2061x-3{csc}^{2}\u2061x \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
